@node Machine collectors
@section Collectors

@table @code
@item local
Runs the statistics script on the machine raspdash runs on (the Pi).

@item ssh
Runs the system @command{ssh} program in batch mode
(@code{BatchMode=yes}, @code{ConnectTimeout=3}) and pipes the
statistics script to @samp{sh -s} on the remote host.  Nothing has to
be installed there.  @command{ssh} reads @file{~/.ssh/config} as usual,
so a host alias works as an @code{address}.  If the key is restricted to
a forced command (@pxref{Adding a host}), the remote side ignores the
piped script and runs the installed copy instead; both ways produce the
same output.  A poll that takes longer than @code{timeout} seconds is
killed.

@item keenetic
Reads a Keenetic router through its web API.  raspdash requests
@file{/auth}, which answers with a realm and a challenge, logs in with
@math{sha256(challenge + md5(login:realm:password))} and then reads
@file{/rci/show/system} (CPU load, memory, uptime) and
@file{/rci/show/version} (model and firmware) with the session cookie.
Keenetic routers report no temperatures.
@end table

The statistics script, @file{agent/raspdash-stats.sh}, is plain POSIX
@command{sh} and needs nothing beyond the base system on GNU/Linux and
macOS.  It prints @samp{key=value} lines; run it by hand to see what a
host reports.  On GNU/Linux temperatures come from
@file{/sys/class/hwmon} (or @file{/sys/class/thermal}); macOS has no
temperature interface without extra tools, so a Mac shows temperatures
only if @command{smctemp} or @command{osx-cpu-temp} is installed.
@command{smctemp} reads CPU and GPU temperatures without root on both
Apple Silicon and Intel Macs; it is not in Homebrew's main repository
but in its author's tap:

@example
brew tap narugit/tap
brew install narugit/tap/smctemp
@end example

@noindent
The script adds @file{/opt/homebrew/bin} and @file{/usr/local/bin} to
@env{PATH} on macOS, because the restricted login the Pi uses does not
read the shell's start-up files.

@node Machine configuration
@section Configuration keys

The @code{[machines]} table:

@table @code
@item interval
Seconds between two polls of a host.  Default 10.
@item timeout
Seconds one poll may take before it is abandoned.  Default 15.
@item history
Number of CPU samples kept for the history chart.  Default 120.
@item ssh_identity
Private key used for @code{ssh} hosts that do not set @code{identity}.
@item stats_script
Path of the statistics script.  By default raspdash looks for
@file{agent/raspdash-stats.sh} next to the configuration file, then in
the installed data directory.
@end table

Each @code{[[machines.hosts]]} table describes one card, in screen
order:

@table @code
@item name
Card title.
@item kind
@code{local}, @code{ssh} or @code{keenetic}.
@item address
Host name, IP address or @file{~/.ssh/config} alias.  Leave it empty
for a placeholder card.
@item user
@itemx port
@itemx identity
Passed to @command{ssh} as @option{-l}, @option{-p} and @option{-i}.
@item ssh_options
Array of extra @option{-o} options for @command{ssh}.
@item credentials
For @code{keenetic}: a TOML file with @code{login} and @code{password}.
@item interval
@itemx timeout
Override the @code{[machines]} values for this host.
@item enabled
Set to @code{false} to show the card without polling the host.
@end table

raspdash remembers what each host is (operating system, kernel, number
of CPUs) in its cache directory, so after a restart the card of an
unreachable host still says what it is.  Measurements are never cached.

@node Adding a host
@section Adding a GNU/Linux or macOS host

The Pi logs in with its own key, @file{~/.ssh/raspdash_ed25519}, which
can be restricted on each host so that it can only print statistics.

@cindex @command{authorize-host.sh}
The quickest way is the helper in the source tree, run from a machine
that can reach both the Pi (as @samp{ssh pi}) and the new host:

@example
deploy/authorize-host.sh user@@192.168.1.44
@end example

@noindent
It asks for the host's password once, installs the statistics script as
@file{~/.local/bin/raspdash-stats} there, adds the Pi's key to
@file{~/.ssh/authorized_keys} with the restrictions shown below (keeping
a backup in @file{authorized_keys.bak-raspdash}), and finally checks
from the Pi that the login works.  @option{-k @var{keyfile}} authorizes
another key, @option{-f @var{address}} sets the address the Pi connects
from (default @samp{192.168.1.96}), and @option{-n} only prints what it
would run on the host.  Then add the host to @file{config.toml}.

The same steps by hand:

@enumerate
@item
On the Pi, create the key once:

@example
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' -C raspdash@@raspberrypi \
    -f ~/.ssh/raspdash_ed25519
@end example

@item
On the new host, make sure an SSH server runs.  On Devuan:
@samp{nala install openssh-server}.  On macOS: System Settings,
General, Sharing, turn on @samp{Remote Login}.

@item
Copy the statistics script to the host:

@example
install -m 755 agent/raspdash-stats.sh ~/.local/bin/raspdash-stats
@end example

@item
Append the Pi's public key (@file{~/.ssh/raspdash_ed25519.pub} on the
Pi) to @file{~/.ssh/authorized_keys} on the host, on one line, prefixed
with restrictions:

@example
command="/home/@var{user}/.local/bin/raspdash-stats",no-pty,
no-port-forwarding,no-agent-forwarding,no-X11-forwarding,
from="192.168.1.96" ssh-ed25519 AAAA@dots{} raspdash@@raspberrypi
@end example

@noindent
(shown wrapped here; it must be a single line).  With
@code{command=}, whatever the Pi asks to run, the host runs only
@file{raspdash-stats}; @code{from=} accepts the key only from the Pi's
address.  On macOS use @file{/Users/@var{user}/@dots{}}.

@item
Check from the Pi:

@example
ssh -i ~/.ssh/raspdash_ed25519 @var{user}@@@var{address}
@end example

@noindent
It must print @samp{agent=1} followed by statistics.  The first
connection records the host key (@code{StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new}).

@item
Set @code{address} and @code{user} of the host's card in
@file{config.toml}.
@end enumerate

Updating the script on a host means copying it again; a host whose key
is not restricted always gets the current script through @samp{sh -s}.

@node Router credentials
@section Router credentials

The @code{keenetic} collector needs the router's web login.  Create
@file{secrets/keenetic.toml} (ignored by git) and make it readable only
by you:

@example
login = "admin"
password = "@var{router password}"
@end example

@example
chmod 600 secrets/keenetic.toml
@end example

A separate router user with read-only rights is better than the admin
account, if the firmware allows it.  Until the file exists, the router's
card says @samp{no router credentials}.

@node Machines internals
@section Internals

This section is for people changing @file{src/machines.cpp}.

@subsubheading Sources and snapshots

Each configured host gets its own @code{HostSource}, named after the
host, with the host's @code{interval}; a failed poll is retried after at
most 30 seconds.  A successful poll publishes a @code{Snapshot} (the
parsed @code{Stats} plus a copy of the CPU history) through
@code{Latest<Snapshot>}.  The history itself is a deque owned by the
poller thread, trimmed to @code{history} samples.  @code{down_since()}
is the start of the current run of failures (the epoch while the host
answers).  Hosts without an address get no source at all.  The screen
decides on every redraw which hosts to show: those with a source whose
status carries no error.

@code{restore()} reloads only the cached @samp{facts} (an
@samp{agent=1} text with hostname, os, kernel and ncpu), publishes them
as a snapshot without measurements, and returns @code{nullopt}, so the
status stays @samp{connecting} (or becomes @samp{offline}) instead of
claiming the host answered.  @code{fetch()} rewrites the facts cache
only when they change, not on every poll.

@subsubheading The statistics format

@code{parse_stats} reads @samp{key=value} lines and requires an
@samp{agent=} line; anything else (for example a shell's @samp{not
found} message) is reported with its first 160 characters.  Byte counts
may arrive in awk's exponent notation (@samp{7.95798e+09}) and are
accepted.  Temperatures at or below 0.5 °C or at 150 °C and above are
dropped: unused hwmon inputs read 0.  Temperatures are sorted hottest
first.  Keys are @samp{temp.@var{chip}.@var{label}}; the script turns
characters other than letters, digits, @samp{_}, @samp{+} and @samp{-}
into @samp{_}, and the screen shows @samp{_} as a space.  A generic
label such as @samp{temp1} is replaced by the chip name.

@subsubheading Child processes

@code{run_command} starts the child with @code{posix_spawnp} (safe in a
multi-threaded program, unlike @code{fork}), with close-on-exec pipes so
concurrent pollers never leak descriptors into each other's children.
It writes the input and reads both outputs through @code{poll} on
non-blocking descriptors, so a child that fills stdout or stderr
cannot deadlock it.  A child that never reads its stdin (a forced
command) makes the write fail with @code{EPIPE}; that is expected, and
raspdash ignores @code{SIGPIPE} process-wide (in
@code{install_machines}) so the failure is reported as an error code
instead of a signal.  When the output is closed but the child has not
exited, it waits until the deadline; at the deadline the child is sent
@code{SIGKILL} and reaped.  @code{status} is the exit status, or -1 when
the child was killed or did not exit normally.

For @code{ssh}, exit status 255 means @command{ssh} itself failed; the
first non-empty line of its stderr, without the @samp{ssh: } prefix,
becomes the error.

@subsubheading Keenetic

A session cookie is kept between polls.  Each poll first tries
@file{/rci/show/system} with it; only an HTTP 401 triggers a new login,
so the password is not sent every ten seconds.  The cookie comes from
the first @samp{Set-Cookie} header (name and value only).  Memory comes
from @code{memtotal}, @code{memfree}, @code{membuffers} and
@code{memcache} (KiB; available = free + buffers + cache); older
firmware only has @code{memory} as @samp{@var{used}/@var{total}} KiB.
@code{uptime} may be a number or a string.  The credentials file is
read at login time, so it can be added without restarting raspdash.

@subsubheading Card layout

The grid uses as few rows of cards as the width allows (cards at least
38 columns wide) and then as few columns as those rows need, which keeps
cards wide.  Inside a card every line is a @code{Row} with a display
@code{order}, a @code{priority} (lower is more important), a height and
an optional @code{grow}.  Rows are picked by priority until the card is
full, drawn in display order, and spare lines go to rows that can grow
(the CPU history chart, up to 10 extra lines).  So a small terminal
keeps the error, CPU, RAM and temperature lines and drops the rest.
The label column is @code{label_w} cells wide: an icon from
@code{tui::icon} and a word.  Data kept from before an outage is drawn
dimmed, and only the hottest temperature gets the good/warn/bad colour;
the others are plain unless they are warm themselves.  The history
chart is a filled Braille area chart, newest sample on the right, with
the time span it covers under the label.
